          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the spaghetti in a large saucepan of well-salted boiling water according to the packet instructions until al dente. Reserve a mugful of pasta water before draining."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While the pasta cooks, heat the vegetable oil in a frying pan over a medium-high heat. Add the salmon chunks and cook for 2 to 3 minutes, turning once, until just cooked through. Break into large flakes with a fork. Remove from the pan and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Take the frying pan off the heat. Add the butter and let it melt in the residual heat. Add the soy sauce and stir to combine. This is the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "soy butter"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the drained pasta to the pan and toss to coat. Add a splash of reserved pasta water if the sauce looks too thick. Fold in the salmon flakes gently."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide between two bowls. Top with the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "nori"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "strips, spring onions and sesame seeds. Serve at once."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Sauce is greasy and split",
              "cause": "The pan was too hot when the butter was added.",
              "fix": "Always take the pan off the heat first. The butter should melt gently. Add a splash of pasta water and toss vigorously to bring the sauce back together."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Salmon is dry and overcooked",
              "cause": "It was left in the pan too long or the heat was too high.",
              "fix": "Salmon needs just 2 to 3 minutes. It should still look slightly translucent in the centre when you remove it. Residual heat will finish it."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Dish is too salty",
              "cause": "Pasta water was heavily salted and the soy sauce added more salt on top.",
              "fix": "Use 1 tablespoon less soy sauce next time. Taste before serving and adjust. Light soy sauce is also less salty than dark."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Wafu pasta became popular in Japan in the 1970s and 1980s as Italian food spread across the country. Japanese cooks began adapting pasta dishes with local ingredients. Soy butter pasta with salmon is one of the most popular versions. It is a weeknight staple in many Japanese households. It is sometimes called wafuu pasta on Japanese restaurant menus, where both Western and Japanese elements appear on the same plate."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}